# Alpaca paper trading. The account is created once and never reset.
AGENT_1_ALPACA_KEY_ID=
AGENT_1_ALPACA_SECRET_KEY=
# The second account. Two agents run the same code on different numbers, which
# is the experiment; one server per account, because it reads its own keys.
AGENT_2_ALPACA_KEY_ID=
AGENT_2_ALPACA_SECRET_KEY=
ALPACA_API_BASE_URL=https://paper-api.alpaca.markets/v2
ALPACA_PAPER=true
# Which of Alpaca's toolsets the agent may see at all.
ALPACA_TOOLSETS=account,trading,assets,options-data,stock-data,news

# How many turns, intents and refusals the page carries. The record is a week
# long by the end; a page is read in one screen.
RECORD_SHOWS=50

# Policy gateway. Every broker call goes through it; the agent never holds broker keys.
# Left empty, the stack points the agents at the envelope stand-in beside them,
# which answers what each is allowed to do and nothing else.
GATEWAY_URL=
# One token per agent, and they must differ: the token is what decides whose
# limits apply, so two agents sharing one would be one agent as far as the rules
# are concerned. Any opaque string will do.
AGENT_1_GATEWAY_TOKEN=
AGENT_2_GATEWAY_TOKEN=
# Which token belongs to which caller, as token=identity pairs. The identities are
# the names in policy/envelope.yaml, and the tokens are the two above.
ENVELOPE_CALLERS=
# Where the session's ORDERS go. Always the gateway, never the broker's own
# server: the gateway is the only thing that can refuse an order and the only
# record that it happened. The session gets this entry point from the entrypoint,
# which writes the address into the session's own MCP configuration.
AGENT_1_BROKER_MCP_URL=
# Where the harness's READS go: prices, the clock, the account, chains. Orders
# must still go through the gateway: it alone can refuse them and record that they
# happened.
#
# Separating the two is required when a user source is declared at the entry
# point.

# Where the PAGE reads the broker. Always the gateway, never the broker's own
# server: what makes the page's credential unable to send an order is the
# gateway refusing it, so this address is what keeps a read-only surface
# read-only.
AGENT_1_PAGE_MCP_URL=

# The screener: what it looks through and what it considers fit to show. The
# analysis is docs/screener.md.
#
# The list must match what the envelope permits (policy/envelope.yaml) - right now
# that is two places holding one fact, and they have diverged before. Comma
# separated.
SCREENER_UNDERLYINGS=SPY,QQQ,IWM,DIA,AAPL,NVDA,TSLA,META,MSFT,AMZN
# The pass's tick. Returned to five minutes on 26 August after a LIVE 429: the
# account's limit of 200 requests a minute is shared between the pass and the
# trading sessions, and in that same hour the sessions began waking twice as often
# (the entry window went from 20m to 10m). A pass costs about 290 requests; on a
# three-minute tick that is ~97 a minute, almost half the limit, and the sessions
# ran short.
# Shortening the tick again is allowed only after MEASURING what the sessions
# spend - otherwise it trades the list's freshness for refusals to the very
# sessions the list exists for.
SCREENER_EVERY=5m
# How many requests a minute the pass takes FOR ITSELF. The broker's limit is 200
# a minute on the free plan and it is shared across the account: the pass divides
# it with the trading sessions. That is why this is not 180. Measured 27 August at
# 180: in the pass's minutes, 133 refusals with 429 out of 187 requests, then 159
# out of 188 - the entry session read neither the news nor the price, and the
# defence twice failed to see the positions and reported the gateway unavailable.
# The number is chosen by measuring refusals, not by reasoning.
SCREENER_KEEP=72h
SCREENER_PER_MINUTE=100
# How far the sold strike sits from the price, in percent.
# Calendar days ahead. Seven, not five: up to five TRADING days stretch to seven
# calendar days across a weekend, and a smaller number hides the far edge.
SCREENER_EXPIRATIONS=7
# Credit to risk: below the first, even a good crossing does not pay for itself;
# above the second it is a broken quote, not generosity.
# How likely the sold strike is to go into the money. A contract without a delta
# does not reach the list: on the expiry day the broker does not compute one, and
# that is exactly the day the strike is crossed most often.
#
# The number must match the ceiling declared in the declaration, and that is not
# decoration. The session ranks by edge_points from read_candidates, that is, it
# chooses FROM the screener's LIST. A threshold here below the declared one means
# the declared one does not work: the declaration permits 0.45 while the sessions
# are never shown anything beyond the threshold. That is how it stood until
# 27 August - 0.45 in the declaration, 0.25 in the screener.
# How many underlyings are asked for AT ONCE. Empty or 0 means one at a time, as
# it used to be. Measured 27 August on live calls through the gateway: one chain
# answer takes about 2.3 seconds (SPY 3.4, AAPL 2.1, XLF 2.0), and half of that is
# the megabyte of the answer, not the connection. One at a time, 284 underlyings
# take ELEVEN minutes and spend 26 requests a minute of the broker's permitted 180.
# What stops the pass should be the broker's limiter, not the shape of our loop.
SCREENER_WORKERS=6
# 0.35, not the 0.30 declared in the declaration: the screener is a NET and the
# session decides. The net must be slightly wider than what is declared, or a
# borderline candidate is lost on the difference between the pass's delta and a
# fresh quote's delta. We already walked into the opposite once: on 27 August the
# declaration permitted 0.45 while the screener cut at 0.25, and the declared value
# had no effect at all.
# A bound on nonsense, not a way of choosing: half the cost of crossing is
# subtracted from the credit before any measurement, so an expensive structure
# shows a low edge by itself. Set it wide. At 20 one pass threw away 833 structures
# against 96 at the next filter.
# By how many percentage points a structure pays MORE than it needs to survive:
# the probability of surviving (1 - |delta|) minus the break-even probability
# priced in. It is computed on the credit AFTER half the cost of crossing is
# subtracted, so the price of entry is inside the measure rather than standing
# beside it as a separate threshold.
# Empty or zero switches the check off. The value is chosen by measurement, not by
# eye; the counter of what was discarded exists for exactly that.
# Zero, not the +2 from the declaration, and this is NOT a measurement but a design
# decision: the session's threshold applies to FRESH quotes, while the pass's
# number has aged by minutes by the time of the decision and is usually higher than
# the fresh one. A net using the session's threshold would throw away exactly those
# candidates that could still reach it. Zero keeps the list readable; the session
# decides.


# Three things used to happen on one tick, at one rate, though they cost three
# different things. Now each has its own interval; all three empty means exactly
# the behaviour this had before they were told apart.
#
# TICK_EVERY - how often the schedule is asked whether anything is due. Asks
# nobody anything: the schedule is a map in memory and no request leaves the
# process. Empty means a minute, which is what the declaration speaks in.
#
# REREAD_EVERY - how often the declaration and the skills are brought level with
# the disk. This is the one that touches the filesystem: it reads every skill
# file twice and parses the mount table, and the cost grows with the skill tree.
# Empty means every tick. SET IT whenever TICK_EVERY goes below a minute, or the
# reading follows the tick - sixty times a minute at a second.
#
# PRICE_EVERY - how often the prices a wake-up watches are read. One request per
# read, out of the two hundred a minute the whole stand shares with the screener,
# the ladder and the agent. Ten seconds is six a minute; one second is thirty, a
# third of the budget spent learning a price nobody asked for. Empty means every
# tick.
#
# A wake-up waiting for a TIME is not held back by PRICE_EVERY: a tick that reads
# no price still fires it.
#
# An interval written as 0 is refused rather than read as "unset": leave it empty
# if you mean unset.
TICK_EVERY=
REREAD_EVERY=
PRICE_EVERY=

# Whose option volatility is recorded all day, and how often. Two of the three
# entry rules compare today's volatility with its own past, and the broker sells
# no such history - this is where it comes from.
VOLATILITY_UNDERLYINGS=SPY,QQQ,IWM,AVGO
VOLATILITY_EVERY=5m

# How often the account's value is written down, how many orders the page
# carries, and how far back its equity line is drawn.
ACCOUNT_EVERY=5m

# The share of the credit received at which a winning structure is bought back,
# and how often the book is looked at for one. ZERO SWITCHES THE WATCH OFF and
# nothing else closes a winner - it is then held to expiry, which is what this
# system did until 28 August 2026 and what one QQQ spread cost it that day. A
# process started with zero says so in its log.
# MEASURED: research/threshold.py, 29 August 2026, on the minute-by-minute path
# of all 553 trades the entry rule selected across 646 days. Holding to expiry
# gives 2,287 dollars and 26% losing trades; closing at 0.35 gives 6,292 and 9%.
TAKE_PROFIT_EVERY=30s

# How the execution ladder walks an unfilled structure: how often it steps, how
# far one step moves the limit, and how long an order may wait before it is
# cancelled. Empty interval means orders rest where the session placed them.
# EXECUTION_EVERY is both the step and the rest: an order sits at each price this
# long before the next move, so the book has time to take it.
EXECUTION_EVERY=45s
EXECUTION_STEP=0.01
EXECUTION_PATIENCE=8m
# How far one step of the ladder moves the limit price. `tick` is one tick a
# step whatever the distance; `arrive` divides what is left to travel by the
# steps left before patience ends, so the walk arrives while the offer still
# stands. Empty is `arrive`. Each agent may override it, and on 1 September the
# the two accounts carry one each - the tactic with arithmetic behind it and
# no live day yet runs on the account standing lower.
EXECUTION_STRIDE=arrive
# How many of the broker's orders are read each pass. It is NOT a display bound:
# the ladder walks, cancels and sums risk from exactly this list, and the entry
# list withholds an underlying that appears in it - so an order pushed out of the
# window is one nothing acts on and nothing counts.
#
# The broker answers with the NEWEST orders of every status, and every price step
# is a replacement, which is a new order record. Measured 31 August: 40 records on
# one account and 25 on the other across the whole day, against a window of
# 25. It did not bite - the pressure inside one 8-minute patience window was about
# one record - but the margin was none.
#
# 200 is four times the busiest day we have run. The structural fix is to ask the
# broker for OPEN orders instead of the newest of all, which takes the bound off
# the correctness path entirely; it is a change to the trading path and waits for
# a closed market.
ORDERS_SHOWN=200
HISTORY_DAYS=14

AGENT_1_DECLARATION=
AGENT_1_PAGE_PORT=8080
AGENT_2_PAGE_PORT=8081

# The chat this session posts to. Leave the token empty and the agent is offered
# no way to post at all.
AGENT_1_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
AGENT_1_TELEGRAM_TOPIC_ID=
# The second agent posts to its own topic in the same chat, so two agents'
# reports do not read as one agent contradicting itself.
AGENT_2_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
AGENT_2_TELEGRAM_TOPIC_ID=
# Whose messages reach a session. Empty means nobody: an open channel into a
# trading agent is not a default.
TELEGRAM_ALLOW_USER_IDS=

# The host's Go module cache, mounted into the database tier: that network has
# no route out, so `go test` there downloads nothing. `go env GOMODCACHE`.
GOMODCACHE=

# Where the Codex login lives on the host. ONE FILE is mounted into the agent -
# `auth.json` and nothing beside it - because the directory it sits in holds
# every session that user has ever run: other projects, other people's work,
# years of transcripts. The model in that container has a shell, and a directory
# it can read is a directory it can be talked into reading. Measured here on
# 29 August: 4.6 GB, 475 session files.
#
# CODEX_AUTH_DIR is where auth.json sits; CODEX_AUTH_FILE names the file itself
# when it is somewhere else. The agent container runs as AGENT_UID so it can read
# it, and that must match the user that owns it on the host.
CODEX_AUTH_DIR=${HOME}/.codex
CODEX_AUTH_FILE=
AGENT_UID=1000
# Empty leaves the agent's own configured model.
AGENT_CALL_TIMEOUT=180s
TURN_LIMIT=6m
SAY_EVERY=25s
# The model a trading session runs on, and how hard it thinks. A session that
# only reads the news names a cheaper model in the declaration.
AGENT_MODEL=
AGENT_REASONING_EFFORT=high

# How the harness reaches the agent, as opposed to which agent it is. Empty is
# `codex`: the agent is started as a child process and spoken to over a pipe.
#
# `mailbox` parks each turn for a client that polls instead - which is how a
# harness a person sits in (Claude Code, and anything else that can run a command
# and read its output) is woken by this same clock, on this same declaration,
# with the same room and the same record. Scripts and instructions: poller/.
#
# With mailbox the address and the token are required and the process refuses to
# start without them: a mailbox served nowhere would park every turn where nobody
# can take it, and that failure looks exactly like a quiet day.
#
# The token IS the identity - it names whose turns those are. One agent, one
# token. Generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32`.
AGENT_DRIVER=
MAILBOX_ADDR=:8090
MAILBOX_TOKEN=
# How long one poll is held open before it answers that there is nothing, and how
# long a parked turn may go unclaimed before it is given up on and said out loud.
# Empty takes 90s and 10m.
MAILBOX_HOLD=
MAILBOX_STALE=

# Database
POSTGRES_USER=agents
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=agents
POSTGRES_DB=agents
# One database per agent, applied by the migrate service in this order. A record
# names the account it belongs to and refuses a process of another, so pointing
# two agents at one name is a startup error and not a silent merge.
RECORD_DATABASES=agents agents_near
# Where `make test-db` builds and drops its own databases.
TEST_DATABASE=postgres://agents:agents@postgres:5432/postgres?sslmode=disable
IMAGE_TAG=latest
# Environment variable names the agent's process is given ON TOP of the ones it
# always gets: PATH, HOME, TMPDIR, LANG, TZ, CODEX_HOME, the proxy variables, and
# the credentials the session's own configuration names - BROKER_MCP_TOKEN,
# AGENT_1_GATEWAY_TOKEN, USER_TOKEN. Everything else the harness holds - the database's
# URL, the chat's bot token, the account keys - does NOT reach the model, because
# it has no use for them and a session that ran `env` would put them in its own
# context. Add a name here when a new server in the session's configuration reads
# its credential from the environment. Comma separated.
AGENT_ENV_KEEP=

# Where the agent goes to reach the outside. Empty means the internet gateway
# beside the stack. A deployment on its own machine does not see that network and
# names its own route, for example http://egress:8888.
AGENT_1_OUTBOUND_PROXY=
AGENT_1_BROKER_MCP_TOKEN=
AGENT_2_BROKER_MCP_URL=
AGENT_2_BROKER_MCP_TOKEN=
# The read-only surface: an observer credential that cannot place an order.
AGENT_1_PAGE_MCP_TOKEN=
AGENT_2_PAGE_MCP_URL=
AGENT_2_PAGE_MCP_TOKEN=
# The model through the policy gateway. Path shape: /byok/{agent}/{instance}/v1
AGENT_1_MODEL_GATEWAY_URL=
AGENT_2_MODEL_GATEWAY_URL=

# A teammate's own Alpaca account. Their keys, sent privately and never to a
# shared chat. With them set, start the server and enable its gateway endpoint:
#   docker compose --profile tikhon up -d alpaca-mcp-tikhon
AGENT_TIKHON_ALPACA_KEY_ID=
AGENT_TIKHON_ALPACA_SECRET_KEY=
# That account's page reads the broker and keeps no record here, so it needs a
# read-only credential and no database.
AGENT_TIKHON_PAGE_MCP_URL=
AGENT_TIKHON_PAGE_MCP_TOKEN=
# A spare account kept for trying a change against the live book without
# touching either judged one.
AGENT_STAND_ALPACA_KEY_ID=
AGENT_STAND_ALPACA_SECRET_KEY=
AGENT_STAND_PAGE_MCP_URL=
AGENT_STAND_PAGE_MCP_TOKEN=

# The hosts THIS deployment in particular needs, as regular expressions separated
# by newlines. The gateway's address goes here when it is not on this machine.
# The domain a judge opens the page on, and the email for the certificate. Only a
# deployment needs them: locally the page answers on its own port.
DASHBOARD_HOST=
ACME_EMAIL=

EXTRA_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# How long resuming yesterday's conversation may take before a fresh one is
# opened instead. It is part of the bound on the whole start, so a value here
# lengthens the time the process gives itself before it gives up.
THREAD_RESUME_LIMIT=20s

# Who the agent acts FOR when it calls the policy gateway. The pair travels
# OUTBOUND, in the gateway's own header beside the machine's credential, so the
# gateway's record reads as "this agent, for this person".
#
# It is not a gate on anything we serve. An earlier version of this comment said
# the page refuses a request without them, which was never true of any line of
# code: `internal/api` authenticates nothing, and the page answers `/api/money`,
# `/api/state` and `/api/equity` to whoever reaches the port. That is the design -
# it is a read side that decides nothing and can only read, and a judge has to be
# able to open it - but it is the operator's job to decide who can reach the port,
# and no line here does it for them.
USER_HEADER_NAME=X-Swiftward-User
USER_TOKEN=

# The key that opens the demo page. It is NOT the pair above: that one travels
# outbound to the gateway. This one is asked of whoever opens the page.
#
# Why it exists. The page is served on a port open to the internet, because the
# platform requires an address a judge opens by hand - and until 1 September it
# asked for nothing, so anyone who reached the port read the account's positions,
# its equity, every order, every intent and the agent's own words, live, during
# the competition. The read side can order nothing and that stays true; what was
# missing was a control on who may READ.
#
# How it arrives: `?key=...` once in the address, after which a cookie carries the
# visit; or the `X-Page-Key` header for a tool. `/healthz` needs none. An empty
# value is REFUSED at startup rather than serving the account to everyone.
#
# The address handed to a judge on submission day is the page's URL with `?key=`.
PAGE_KEY=
